%[text] # AI Verification: Proving Neural Network Robustness
%[text] This script demonstrates how to verify that neural networks behave correctly using formal methods from the [AI Verification Library](https://www.mathworks.com/products/ai-verification.html). At the edge, mistakes are expensive -- once an AI model is optimized, compiled, and connected to real sensors, failures are harder to diagnose. Formal verification provides mathematical guarantees about model behavior that go beyond what testing alone can achieve.
%[text] The script covers three progressively more rigorous approaches using the ACAS Xu (Airborne Collision Avoidance System for unmanned aircraft) neural networks:
%[text] 1. *Explore the network* -- understand what the ACAS Xu system does, how its neural networks make predictions, and observe advisory transitions as aircraft separation changes.
%[text] 2. *Verify local robustness* -- use the CROWN and $\\alpha$-CROWN algorithms to formally prove (or disprove) that the network's advisory is stable under bounded input perturbations at a single operating point.
%[text] 3. *Verify global stability via adaptive mesh* -- scale verification across the entire operational design domain, producing a verification map and quantifying how much of the input space is formally proven stable. \
%[text] Based on the example [Verify and Deploy ACAS Xu Neural Networks](https://www.mathworks.com/help/deeplearning/ug/verify-and-deploy-acas-xu-neural-networks.html).
%%
%[text] ## Setup
%[text] Verify that the required toolboxes and add-ons are installed, and prepare the models. If this is your first time running the exercise, run `helper.setupModels` to download the ACAS Xu neural networks.
if ~isfolder("model"), mkdir("models"), end;
if isempty(dir(fullfile("models", "*.mat"))) 
    addpath("models")
    helper.setupModels;
end
%%
%[text] ## Stage 1: Explore ACAS Xu Neural Networks
%[text] ACAS Xu (Airborne Collision Avoidance System for unmanned aircraft) uses neural networks to recommend steering advisories that prevent mid-air collisions. The original system used a numeric lookup table requiring ~2 GB of storage. By replacing each table with a small neural network, the full system fits in ~3 MB -- a critical advantage for resource-constrained unmanned platforms.
%[text] The system comprises **45 neural networks**, one for each combination of previous advisory (5 values) and discretized time-until-loss-of-vertical-separation $\\tau$ (9 values). 
%[text] - $\\tau \\;${"editStyle":"visual"}: Time until loss of vertical separation (seconds). Values: `[0, 1, 5, 10, 20, 40, 60, 80, 100]`
%[text] - $a\_{\\mathrm{prev}}${"editStyle":"visual"}: Previous advisory. Values: `[Clear-of-Conflict, Weak Left, Weak Right, Strong Left, Strong Right]` \
%[text] Each network takes **5 inputs** describing the positions and velocities of the ownship and intruder ship:
%[text] - $\\rho${"editStyle":"visual"} : Distance from ownship to intruder (feet). Range/Values: \[0, 60760\]
%[text] - $\\theta \\;${"editStyle":"visual"}: Angle to intruder relative to ownship heading direction, counterclockwise (radians). Range/Values: \[$-\\pi${"editStyle":"visual"}, $\\pi${"editStyle":"visual"}\]
%[text] - $\\psi${"editStyle":"visual"}: Heading angle of intruder relative to ownship heading direction, counterclockwise (radians). Range/Values: \[$-\\pi${"editStyle":"visual"}, $\\pi${"editStyle":"visual"}\]
%[text] - $v\_{own}$: Speed of ownship (ft/s). Range/Values: \[100, 1200\]
%[text] - $v\_{int}$ : Speed of intruder (ft/s). Range/Values: \[0, 1200\] \
%[text] The network outputs scores for **5 advisories**: Clear-of-Conflict (no action needed), Weak Left/Right (gentle turn), or Strong Left/Right (urgent maneuver). The advisory with the highest score is selected.
%[text] ![Encounter geometry showing the five ACAS Xu inputs: horizontal distance rho, angles theta and psi, and velocities v_own and v_int.](text:image:ac01)
%%
%[text] ### Load the ACAS Xu Network
%[text] Load the network for previous advisory = 1 (Clear-of-Conflict) and $\\tau = 1$ (imminent collision). This is one of 45 networks in the ACAS Xu system.
previousAdvisory = 1;
tau = 1;
acasNet = helper.loadACASNetwork(previousAdvisory, tau);

advisoryNames = helper.classNames;
disp("Advisory classes: " + join(string(advisoryNames), ", "))
%%
%[text] ### Predict an Advisory
%[text] "Left Abeam" scenario:
%[text] ![](text:image:59ca)
%[text] Consider the "Left Abeam" scenario: two aircraft separated by 15,000 ft, with the intruder directly to the left ($\\theta = \\pi/2$). The ownship flies at 954.6 ft/s and the intruder at 1050 ft/s with a slight relative heading offset ($\\psi = -0.43$ rad). Predict the advisory at this single operating point.
rho0 = 15000;
theta = pi/2;
psi = -0.4296;
vOwn = 954.6;
vInt = 1050;

X0 = dlarray([rho0 theta psi vOwn vInt], "BC");
scores = predict(acasNet, X0);
nominalLabel = scores2label(scores, advisoryNames)
%[text] The network recommends **Weak Right** -- a gentle turn away from the intruder. But what happens as the aircraft get closer?
%%
%[text] ### Simulate an Encounter Trajectory
%[text] Simulate 100 seconds of straight-line flight (open-loop -- both aircraft maintain heading regardless of advisory). As time progresses, the horizontal separation decreases. At each timestep, query the network for its recommended advisory and observe how the advisory changes as the collision threat evolves.
t = (0:99)';
numSteps = numel(t);

% Ownship trajectory (flying north along y-axis)
xOwn = zeros(numSteps, 1);
yOwn = vOwn * t;

% Intruder trajectory (starting at the Left Abeam position)
xInt0 = rho0 * sin(theta);
yInt0 = rho0 * cos(theta);
xInt = xInt0 + vInt * sin(-psi) * t;
yInt = yInt0 + vInt * cos(-psi) * t;

% Compute separation at each timestep
rhoTraj = sqrt((xInt - xOwn).^2 + (yInt - yOwn).^2);

% Predict advisory at each timestep
XTraj = repmat([rho0, theta, psi, vOwn, vInt], [numSteps, 1]);
XTraj(:,1) = rhoTraj;
YTraj = predict(acasNet, dlarray(XTraj, "BC"));
advisoryTraj = scores2label(YTraj, advisoryNames);
%[text] ### Visualize Advisory Transitions
%[text] Plot the horizontal separation and the network's advisory over time. As the aircraft converge, the advisory transitions from Clear-of-Conflict to increasingly urgent maneuver recommendations. These transitions define the *decision boundaries* of the network -- the regions in input space where the advisory changes.
figure
tiledlayout(2,1)

nexttile
plot(t, rhoTraj, LineWidth=1.5)
ylabel("Separation \rho (ft)")
title("Encounter Trajectory — Left Abeam Configuration")
grid on

nexttile
advisoryIdx = double(advisoryTraj);
scatter(t, advisoryIdx, 20, advisoryIdx, "filled")
yticks(1:5)
yticklabels(string(advisoryNames))
ylim([0.5 5.5])
xlabel("Time (s)")
ylabel("Advisory")
title("Network Advisory Over Time")
colormap(helper.getClassColormapMatrix)
grid on
%[text] The network transitions between advisories as the encounter evolves -- from Clear-of-Conflict when the aircraft are far apart, to progressively more urgent maneuvers as separation decreases. These advisory transitions are the decision boundaries that formal verification will analyze.
%[text] **But how confident can we be in these transitions?** Testing individual points tells us what the network predicts *at those points*, but says nothing about what happens between them. A small change in velocity or angle might push the network to a different advisory. *Formal verification* answers this question with mathematical certainty.
%%
%[text] ## Stage 2: Verify Local Robustness
%[text] Instead of testing individual inputs, formal verification proves that *every* input within a bounded region produces the same output. The `verifyNetworkRobustness` function uses the CROWN algorithm to compute an overapproximation of the network's reachable output set and determines whether the predicted class is guaranteed to dominate.
%[text] The [`verifyNetworkRobustness`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/releases/R2026a/deeplearning/ref/verifynetworkrobustness.html) function checks whether the network classifies all outputs for a given input range as the same class. The function has three possible outputs:
%[text] - `verified` — The network is robust to adversarial inputs between the specified bounds.
%[text] - `violated` — The network is not robust to adversarial inputs between the specified bounds.
%[text] - `unproven` — The function is unable to prove whether the network is robust to adversarial inputs between the specified bounds. \
%[text] ![](text:image:49ca)
%[text] ### Define the Operating Point
%[text] Return to the Left Abeam scenario at the original separation of 15,000 ft. The expected advisory is Weak Right (class 3).
X = [15000 pi/2 -0.4296 954.6 1050];
label = 3; % Weak Right
%%
%[text] ### Small Perturbation: Verified
%[text] Allow the velocity inputs (features 4 and 5) to vary by $\\pm 3%$ while keeping geometric inputs fixed. With this small perturbation, the verifier should return `"verified"`, formally proving the network is robust -- *every* input in this region produces the same advisory.
dX = 0.03;
xLower = X .* [1 1 1 1-dX 1-dX];
xUpper = X .* [1 1 1 1+dX 1+dX];

xLower = dlarray(xLower, "BC");
xUpper = dlarray(xUpper, "BC");

result = verifyNetworkRobustness(acasNet, xLower, xUpper, label) %#ok<*NASGU>
%%
%[text] ### Larger Perturbation: Unproven
%[text] Increase the velocity perturbation to $\\pm 8%$. With a wider input range, the CROWN algorithm's overapproximation of nonlinear (ReLU) layers may produce output bounds that are too loose to separate the correct class from all others. An `"unproven"` result does *not* mean the network is wrong -- it means the algorithm cannot determine the answer with the current precision.
dX = 0.08;
xLower = dlarray(X .* [1 1 1 1-dX 1-dX], "BC");
xUpper = dlarray(X .* [1 1 1 1+dX 1+dX], "BC");

[result, detailedResult] = verifyNetworkRobustness(acasNet, xLower, xUpper, label) %#ok<*ASGLU>
%[text] For the classes `Weak Left` and `Strong Left`, the function returns `verified`. This means that the network does not predict either of these classes anywhere within the input bounds. This makes sense, since the expected outcome is for the advisory to tell the aircraft to turn right.
%%
%[text] ### **Refine Verification by Partitioning Input Region**
%[text] To reduce the amount of overapproximation of the output bounds, split the input region spanned by `XLower` and `XUpper` into several smaller input regions, then attempt to verify the network robustness on each of the subregions.
%[text] ![](text:image:63b5)
%[text] The figure shows how bisecting the input region can tighten the output bounds. 
%[text] Bisect the region along the $v\_{\\int }${"editStyle":"visual"} range of \[966 1134\] into \[966 1050\] and \[1050 1134\].
xLower = repmat(xLower,1,2);
xUpper = repmat(xUpper,1,2);

xLower(5,2) = 1050;
xUpper(5,1) = 1050;
label  = [3 3];
%[text] Verify the robustness of these two subregions.
verifyNetworkRobustness(acasNet,xLower,xUpper,label)
%%
%[text] ### Refine with the $\\alpha$-CROWN Algorithm
%[text] The $\\alpha$-CROWN variant fine-tunes polyhedral constraints at ReLU layers to produce tighter output bounds at the cost of longer computation. Re-run verification on the **full** $\\pm 8%$ bounds (without partitioning) using `Algorithm="alpha-crown"`.
dX = 0.08;
xLower = dlarray(X .* [1 1 1 1-dX 1-dX], "BC");
xUpper = dlarray(X .* [1 1 1 1+dX 1+dX], "BC");
label = 3;
resultAlpha = verifyNetworkRobustness(acasNet, xLower, xUpper, label, Algorithm="alpha-crown")
%%
%[text] ### Compare Output Bounds: CROWN vs. $\\alpha$-CROWN
%[text] The [`estimateNetworkOutputBounds`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/releases/R2026a/deeplearning/ref/estimatenetworkoutputbounds.html) function computes the lower and upper bound of the output values that a network returns when the input is between specified input lower and upper bounds.
%[text] Visualize the output score bounds from both algorithms side-by-side. The $\\alpha$-CROWN bounds (red) are noticeably tighter than the CROWN bounds (blue). For CROWN, the bounds for Weak Right may overlap with other classes, preventing proof. For $\\alpha$-CROWN, there is no overlap.
[YLowerCROWN, YUpperCROWN] = estimateNetworkOutputBounds(acasNet, xLower, xUpper);
[YLowerAlpha, YUpperAlpha] = estimateNetworkOutputBounds(acasNet, xLower, xUpper, Algorithm="alpha-crown");

helper.displayBoundComparison(YLowerCROWN, YUpperCROWN, YLowerAlpha, YUpperAlpha)
%%
%[text] ### A Genuinely Non-Robust Case
%[text] Increase the velocity perturbation to $\\pm 25%$. At this scale, the input range covers physically different scenarios -- a very slow intruder may not pose a collision threat, causing the network to legitimately change its advisory. Neither algorithm can verify robustness.
dX = 0.25;
label = 3;
xLower = dlarray(X .* [1 1 1 1-dX 1-dX], "BC");
xUpper = dlarray(X .* [1 1 1 1+dX 1+dX], "BC");
xUpper(4:5) = min(xUpper(4:5), 1200);

[result, detailedResult] = verifyNetworkRobustness(acasNet, xLower, xUpper, label, Algorithm="alpha-crown")
%%
%[text] The function returns `unproven` for all classes.
%[text] To prove that the network is not robust within this large input range, find an example input for which the network does not advise `Weak Right` by using the [`findAdversarialExamples`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/releases/R2026a/deeplearning/ref/findadversarialexamples.html) function. The `findAdversarialExamples` finds adversarial examples starting from a random guess. Run the function repeatedly, up to 100 times or until it finds an adversarial example.
for ii = 1:100
    [xAdversarial,mislabel,iX] = findAdversarialExamples(acasNet,xLower,xUpper,label);
    if ~isempty(xAdversarial)
        disp("Adversial example found after " + ii + " attempts.")
        disp(mat2str(extractdata(xAdversarial)',4))
        advisory = helper.classNames(mislabel)
        break
    end
end
%%
%[text] ## Stage 3: Verify Global Stability Using Adaptive Mesh
%[text] In Stage 2, you verified robustness at a single operating point. But safety-critical systems must be verified across their *entire* operational envelope. A uniform grid approach wastes computation: most of the input space is far from decision boundaries and verifies easily, while boundary regions need fine resolution.
%[text] The **adaptive mesh** algorithm starts with the entire operational design domain (ODD) as one hypercuboid. If verification succeeds, the region is marked stable. If unproven, the hypercuboid is bisected along its widest angular dimension and each half is re-verified. This continues until all regions are verified or the minimum resolution is reached.
%[text] ![](text:image:949b)
%%
%[text] ### Configure the Operational Design Domain
%[text] Fix the distance ($\n\\rho = 15{,}000$ ft) and velocities ($v\_{own} = 954.6$ ft/s, $v\_{int} = 1050$ ft/s) while sweeping the two heading angles ($\\theta$ and $\\psi$) across the full range $\[-\\pi, \\pi\]$. The `minAngle` parameter (in radians) prevents infinite subdivision. Larger `miniBatchSize` values use GPU memory more efficiently.
minAngle = 0.1;
miniBatchSize = 4096;

rho = 15000;

thetaLower = -pi;
thetaUpper = pi;
psiLower = -pi;
psiUpper = pi;

oddLower = [rho thetaLower psiLower vOwn vInt];
oddUpper = [rho thetaUpper psiUpper vOwn vInt];
%%
%[text] ### Initialize Data Structures
%[text] Create the initial hypercuboid spanning the entire ODD, a queue for processing, and storage for verified results. The `helper.CubeBatch` class holds collections of hypercuboids and the `helper.CubeBatchQueue` manages the FIFO processing queue.
oddLower = dlarray(oddLower, "BC");
oddUpper = dlarray(oddUpper, "BC");

cubes = helper.CubeBatch(oddLower, oddUpper);
verifiedCubes = helper.CubeBatch;
cubeQueue = helper.CubeBatchQueue;
enqueue(cubeQueue, cubes);

numHypercuboids = 0;
verifiedLabelBatch = [];
hypercuboidCenters = [];
helper.adaptiveMeshProgressMonitor;
%%
%[text] ### Run the Adaptive Mesh Verification Loop
%[text] The main loop repeatedly: (1) retrieves a batch of hypercuboids from the queue, (2) predicts the advisory at each center, (3) calls `verifyNetworkRobustness` to check if the predicted advisory holds across the entire region, (4) stores verified regions and bisects unproven ones.
%[text] Watch the figure update in real time. Colored rectangles represent verified regions, with each color corresponding to an advisory class. The algorithm concentrates subdivisions near decision boundaries. With `minAngle=0.1`, this should complete in 1-3 minutes.
while ~isempty(cubeQueue)
    cubes = getCubes(cubeQueue, miniBatchSize);

    centers = getCenters(cubes);
    hypercuboidCenters = [hypercuboidCenters extractdata(centers)]; %#ok<AGROW>

    advisory = predict(acasNet, centers);
    [~, idxLabel] = max(extractdata(advisory));

    results = verifyNetworkRobustness(acasNet, cubes.xLower, cubes.xUpper, idxLabel, MiniBatchSize=miniBatchSize);
    idxVerified = (results == "verified");
    idxUnproven = (results == "unproven");

    verifiedMiniBatch = getSubset(cubes, idxVerified);
    verifiedLabel = idxLabel(idxVerified);
    verifiedLabelBatch = [verifiedLabelBatch verifiedLabel]; %#ok<AGROW>
    verifiedCubes = addSubset(verifiedCubes, verifiedMiniBatch);

    xlVerified = extractdata(verifiedMiniBatch.xLower);
    xuVerified = extractdata(verifiedMiniBatch.xUpper);
    sideLengths = xuVerified - xlVerified;
    for kk = 1:getNumCubes(verifiedMiniBatch)
        hold on
        rectangle( ...
            Position=[xlVerified(2,kk) xlVerified(3,kk) sideLengths(2,kk) sideLengths(3,kk)], ...
            FaceColor=[helper.getClassColors(verifiedLabel(kk)) 0.05], ...
            EdgeColor="none");
    end
    hold off
    drawnow

    unprovenCubes = getSubset(cubes, idxUnproven);
    helper.bisectAndUpdateQueue(cubeQueue, unprovenCubes, minAngle);

    numHypercuboids = numHypercuboids + getNumCubes(cubes);
end
%%
%[text] ### Analyze Sample Points
%[text] Verify that the network predictions at specific sample points match the verified regions in the plot. Each sample point should produce an advisory that matches the color of the region it falls in.
samplePoints = [
    [rho -3.00  3.00 vOwn vInt];  % Clear-of-Conflict
    [rho -1.50  1.00 vOwn vInt];  % Strong Left
    [rho -0.78  2.10 vOwn vInt];  % Weak Left
    [rho -0.78  1.57 vOwn vInt];  % Strong Right
    [rho -0.39  1.97 vOwn vInt];  % Weak Right
    [rho  0.70 -1.57 vOwn vInt];  % Strong Left
    [rho  0.78 -0.78 vOwn vInt];  % Weak Left
    [rho  0.90 -1.57 vOwn vInt];  % Strong Right
    [rho  1.57 -0.78 vOwn vInt];  % Weak Right
    [rho  0.95 -1.97 vOwn vInt];  % Strong Right
    ];

advisory = predict(acasNet, samplePoints);
sampleAdvisory = scores2label(advisory, advisoryNames)
%[text] The class labels in each region match those from the global stability analysis.
%%
%[text] ### Compute Verification Coverage
%[text] Calculate what fraction of the ODD was formally verified as stable. With `minAngle=0.1`, a high proportion of the ODD should be verified (typically \>90%). The unverified regions concentrate at decision boundaries.
totalVolume = prod(oddUpper(2:3) - oddLower(2:3));
verifiedVolume = sum(prod(verifiedCubes.xUpper(2:3,:) - verifiedCubes.xLower(2:3,:)));

disp("Stable proportion of ODD: " + extractdata(verifiedVolume / totalVolume));
disp("Number of hypercuboids: " + numHypercuboids);
%[text] ### Visualize Class Proportions
%[text] Plot a bar chart showing how much of the ODD belongs to each advisory class, plus the unverified fraction. Clear-of-Conflict typically dominates the ODD. The unverified fraction is small and corresponds to decision boundaries.
helper.plotClassProportion(verifiedLabelBatch, verifiedCubes, totalVolume)
%[text] ### Visualize Hypercuboid Density
%[text] Plot a 2D histogram of hypercuboid center locations. Dense clusters indicate where the algorithm performed the most subdivisions -- high density along decision boundaries, low density in the stable interior of each advisory region.
helper.plotHypercuboidDensity(hypercuboidCenters)
%%
%[text] ## Export Results
%[text] Uncomment the lines below to export this script as an HTML report.
%if ~isfolder("results"), mkdir("results"); end
%export("AIVerification.m", fullfile("results", "AIVerification.html"));
%%
%[text] ## Summary
%[text] This exercise demonstrated three progressively rigorous approaches to AI verification, all using the ACAS Xu collision avoidance system:
%[text] 1. **Exploration** revealed that the network transitions between advisories as encounter geometry changes. These decision boundaries are the critical regions where verification matters most.
%[text] 2. **Local robustness verification** used formal methods (CROWN and $\\alpha$-CROWN) to provide mathematical guarantees about network behavior within bounded input regions. The three possible outcomes -- *verified*, *unproven*, and *violated* -- give precise information about robustness and the effectiveness of different algorithms.
%[text] 3. **Adaptive mesh verification** scaled formal methods to the entire operational design domain. The algorithm concentrates computation on decision boundaries while quickly verifying stable interior regions, achieving high coverage efficiently. \
%[text] **Try this:** Re-run the adaptive mesh loop with `minAngle = 0.05` for higher resolution. How does the verified proportion change? How many more hypercuboids are processed
%%
